.Dd October 25, 2026
.Dt SYSCONF-UCL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm "sysconf ucl"
.Nd read and modify a UCL file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm sysconf
.Cm ucl
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl ceinNqvx
.Op Fl j Ar jail | Fl R Ar dir
.Ar name Ns Op Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Cm ucl
target of
.Xr sysconf 8
reads and rewrites files in the Universal Configuration Language,
the syntax
.Xr pkg.conf 5
documents.
The files are parsed and emitted by
.Xr libucl 3 ,
the same private library
.Xr pkg 8
links, so what this target reads is what
.Xr pkg 8
reads, including any defect the two then share.
Like
.Cm generic ,
the target has no default files of its own and always requires
.Fl f .
The
.Cm pkg
target is this format with the
.Xr pkg 8
file collection bolted on;
see
.Xr sysconf-pkg 8 .
.Pp
Each file is parsed on its own, in order, and every scalar is reported
under its dotted path:
the key
.Ql enabled
inside the object
.Ql FreeBSD-ports
is
.Ql FreeBSD-ports.enabled ,
and objects nest as
.Ql a.b.c .
The last file to define a path is authoritative, as for every other
target.
Arrays are leaves and read back as compact JSON.
On an array,
.Ar name Ns += Ns Ar word
and
.Ar name Ns -= Ns Ar word
add and strike elements
.Pq each word parsed as a value, so Ql 3 is a number
rather than words of a string;
the survivors keep their order and newcomers follow the last of them,
one per line when the array is laid out that way.
On a string they are the word edits of
.Xr sysconf 8 .
.Pp
Under an array a path component is an index rather than a key, as
.Xr uclcmd 1
spells it:
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i
is one element, counting from zero
.Po or from the end when
.Ar i
is negative, so
.Ql -1
is the last
.Pc ,
and
.Ar name Ns \&.#
the number of elements
.Pq reported under the name of the array counted .
A negative index is reported as the index it resolved to.
Under an object the same components are keys, so
.Ql errors.404
reads the key
.Ql 404 .
Indices chain into nested arrays:
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i Ns \&. Ns Ar j
is an element of the array that is element
.Ar i ,
and
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i Ns \&.#
its count.
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i Ns = Ns Ar value
changes that element where it stands, or appends when
.Ar i
is the count;
.Ar name Ns \&.#= Ns Ar value
appends likewise, and reports the index the new element took.
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i Ns += Ns Ar word
and
.Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i Ns -= Ns Ar word
edit the element as they would a value of its kind,
words on a string and elements on an array;
.Fl x Ar name Ns \&. Ns Ar i
strikes it and, like
.Ql -= ,
reports the array before and after.
Requests in one run see one another's edits, though a path into an
array is only read as indices once the array exists in a file.
An index past either end reads as an unknown directive, a value that is
not an array cannot be indexed, and the count can be neither removed
nor edited with
.Ql +=
or
.Ql -= .
A key that itself contains
.Ql \&.
cannot be addressed.
A key the library cannot take bare
.Po such as
.Ql *
or
.Ql [1m
.Pc
is written quoted;
a file that leaves such a key bare
.Po as
.Xr uclcmd 1
may write
.Ql *
.Pc
is still read.
Values are shown as the library renders them:
a boolean written
.Ql yes
reads back
.Ql true ,
and
.Ql 1kb
reads back
.Ql 1024 .
.Pp
A value given on the command line is parsed as UCL,
so
.Ql yes ,
.Ql 10 ,
.Ql "[ a, b ]" ,
and
.Ql "{ k = v }"
land with the types
.Xr pkg 8
would read.
A value that does not parse to exactly the text given
.Pq a Ql # the grammar would take for a comment, for instance
is stored as that literal string, quoted.
Missing intermediate objects are created on a write;
a write through a key that is not an object is an error.
.Pp
A write changes only the bytes it must.
The statement is located in the file and its value replaced in place,
in the file's own style:
the separator
.Pq Ql \&: or Ql = ,
the terminator
.Pq Ql \&, or Ql \&; ,
quoting, indentation, and even the spelling of a boolean
.Pq a Ql yes becomes Ql no , a Ql true becomes Ql false
are taken from what is already there.
A value is written as typed
.Pq Ql 1kb stays Ql 1kb ;
a string is quoted when the file quoted it or when it could not stand bare;
a key that cannot stand bare is quoted even when its neighbours are not.
A new key is added after its siblings in their style;
a new object is appended in the style of the objects already in the file.
An array that only gains or loses elements has those elements added or
taken out one at a time, in the style of the elements around them;
one that is reordered is replaced whole.
Removing a key takes its own lines, a comment trailing it on the last of
them included, and, when that leaves the enclosing object empty, the
object too;
striking an array element does the same with its line.
A file that nothing changed is not rewritten;
one that is, is replaced atomically with its mode kept.
.Pp
The library has the last word.
After an edit the result is parsed again with
.Xr libucl 3
and the write is refused, leaving the file untouched,
unless the parsed contents differ from the original in exactly the
requested key.
Macros such as
.Ql .include
are not expanded, so a parse never pulls in another file,
and no variables are defined, so
.Ql ${ABI}
stays literal in both directions.
A key repeated in one file is an implicit array to the library,
which presents its first element;
that is the value reported and the statement a write edits.
.Pp
.Xr libucl 3
keeps no line numbers; the trails shown by
.Fl V
report line 0.
Shared options are in
.Xr sysconf 8 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf server.port
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf server.port=8080
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf 'server.hosts=[ a, b ]'
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf server.hosts+=c 'server.hosts.#'
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf 'server.hosts.#=d' server.hosts.-1
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf -x server.hosts.0
.Dl sysconf ucl -f /tmp/app.conf matrix.1.0=30 'matrix.1.#'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr uclcmd 1 Pq Pa ports/textproc/uclcmd ,
.Xr libucl 3 ,
.Xr pkg.conf 5 ,
.Xr sysconf 8 ,
.Xr sysconf-generic 8 ,
.Xr sysconf-pkg 8 ,
